The error you're encountering typically happens when the state being accessed is not properly initialized. Let's check the state initialization in 'src/stateManager.js'. Here's a snippet:

```javascript
class StateManager {
    constructor() {
        this.currentState = {};
    }

    updateState(newState) {
        this.currentState = { ...this.currentState, ...newState };
    }
}
```

Make sure that you are creating an instance of `StateManager` correctly before accessing `currentState`. If you are trying to access it within your tests, ensure your setup looks similar to this:

```javascript
const stateManager = new StateManager();
expect(stateManager.currentState).toEqual({});
```

After making any necessary adjustments, don't forget to run your tests with Jest to ensure everything is functioning correctly. Use the command `npm test` in your terminal.
